1
00:00:41,080 --> 00:00:45,660
CONVERSATION 1
Wow, I would give anything to be more like Audrey Hepburn.

2
00:00:45,660 --> 00:00:50,900
I never really understood why so many girls were such big fans of her.

3
00:00:50,900 --> 00:01:00,900
I mean, I've seen the famous films, Roman Holiday, Breakfast At Tiffany's and a few others, but I still don't fully get it.

4
00:01:00,900 --> 00:01:03,260
Was she that great of an actress?

5
00:01:03,260 --> 00:01:07,720
Well, for me, my adoration goes beyond her movies.

6
00:01:07,720 --> 00:01:11,480
She had such a classic elegance about her.

7
00:01:11,480 --> 00:01:20,300
She was always so poised, in part because she spent years training as a ballet dancer before becoming an actress.

8
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:22,640
Why didn't she stick to dancing as a career?

9
00:01:22,640 --> 00:01:25,160
It seems it was fate.

10
00:01:25,160 --> 00:01:34,440
She suffered from inadequate nutrition during the war, and therefore, a career as a professional dancer would have been too demanding on her body.

11
00:01:34,440 --> 00:01:37,660
So she focused on acting instead.

12
00:01:37,660 --> 00:01:42,300
Roman Holiday was her first big break which made her a star.

13
00:01:42,300 --> 00:01:47,320
Was that the film that opened with her shopping for jewelry in New York city?

14
00:01:47,320 --> 00:01:54,900
You know, the scene she was wearing a black dress and dark sunglasses with a pearl necklace and long black gloves.

15
00:01:54,900 --> 00:01:57,740
I see the photo of her in that costume everywhere.

16
00:01:57,740 --> 00:02:01,200
No, that one is Breakfast At Tiffany's.

17
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:07,740
That costume is often referred to as the most famous little black dress of all time.

18
00:02:07,740 --> 00:02:17,660
Her character in that film is very outgoing and charming, even though in real life, Audrey always described herself as shy and quiet.

19
00:02:17,660 --> 00:02:21,480
So what did she do after her acting career?

20
00:02:21,480 --> 00:02:25,420
She dedicated much of her life to helping children in need.

21
00:02:25,420 --> 00:02:30,640
Her family received international aid during the war when she was growing up.

22
00:02:30,640 --> 00:02:33,580
I think that left a big impression on her.

23
00:02:33,580 --> 00:02:38,840
That's where I got the idea to volunteer for children's charity next weekend.

24
00:02:38,840 --> 00:02:40,280
I'll join you.

25
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:53,460
I may not be as charming as Audrey Hepburn, but I'm all for supporting a good cause.

26
00:02:53,460 --> 00:03:10,880
Q1. What does the man say he never really understood?

27
00:03:10,880 --> 00:03:29,900
Q2. What prevented Audrey Hepburn from becoming a professional dancer?

28
00:03:29,900 --> 00:03:48,220
Q3. What do we learn about Audrey Hepburn in real life?

29
00:03:48,220 --> 00:04:08,940
Q4. Why did Audrey Hepburn devote much of her life to charity after her acting career?

30
00:04:08,940 --> 00:04:13,720
CONVERSATION 2
So how is our presentation about the restructuring of the company coming along?

31
00:04:13,720 --> 00:04:20,780
Fine, I am putting the finishing touches to it now, but we will have to be prepared for questions.

32
00:04:20,780 --> 00:04:28,580
Yes, there is already a feeling that this is a top-down change, we really need to get everyone on board.

33
00:04:28,580 --> 00:04:32,060
Well, there's been an extensive consultation period.

34
00:04:32,060 --> 00:04:36,960
I know, but there is always the feeling that if it isn't broken, don't fix it.

35
00:04:36,960 --> 00:04:39,840
People are worried about their jobs too.

36
00:04:39,840 --> 00:04:46,640
I think we need to address that while there will be some job changes, there won't be anyone getting dismissed.

37
00:04:46,640 --> 00:04:49,620
In fact, we are looking to take on more staff.

38
00:04:49,620 --> 00:04:50,700
Agreed.

39
00:04:50,700 --> 00:04:53,540
You can hardly blame people for worrying, though.

40
00:04:53,540 --> 00:04:58,160
We need to make it clear that it's not just change for change's sake.

41
00:04:58,160 --> 00:05:03,060
In other words, we really must make the case for why we are doing it.

42
00:05:03,060 --> 00:05:06,700
So, what's the outline of the presentation?

43
00:05:06,700 --> 00:05:15,280
I'll start with a brief review of the reasons for the change that we really need to make a clean break to restart growth.

44
00:05:15,280 --> 00:05:20,800
After that, I will outline the new company structures and who is going where.

45
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:28,420
Then I will hand it over to you to discuss the timeline and summarize, and we will take questions together at the end.

46
00:05:28,420 --> 00:05:29,460
Anything else?

47
00:05:29,460 --> 00:05:39,620
Oh, yeah, we should let the staff know the channels of communication, you know, who they can contact or direct questions to about these changes.

48
00:05:39,620 --> 00:05:46,160
Yes, and we can collect some frequently asked questions and present some general answers.

49
00:05:46,160 --> 00:06:00,520
Hmm, and will the presentation be available on the company network?

50
00:06:00,520 --> 00:06:07,255
Yes, we are putting it up on the website tomorrow morning, so staff can review the details themselves.

51
00:06:07,255 --> 00:06:08,752
Good.

52
00:06:08,752 --> 00:06:12,120
I think that will answer most of their concerns.

53
00:06:12,120 --> 00:06:28,640
Q5. What is the man doing now?

54
00:06:28,640 --> 00:06:46,540
Q6. What does the man say about the restructuring?

55
00:06:46,540 --> 00:07:03,420
Q7. What will the man explain first in the presentation?

56
00:07:03,420 --> 00:07:52,760
Q8. How can the staff learn more about the company's restructuring?

57
00:07:52,760 --> 00:08:05,120
PASSAGE 1
Airline passengers have to deal with a lot these days, getting bumped from flights and losing luggage on top of the general anxiety that nervous passengers always feel.

58
00:08:05,120 --> 00:08:15,060
At the Cincinnati/Northern Kentucky International Airport, miniature horses deliver a calming force two times a month.

59
00:08:15,060 --> 00:08:22,620
Denver and Ruby are two of the 34 therapy horses brought in from a local farm.

60
00:08:22,620 --> 00:08:28,840
They can usually be found in the ticket counter area interacting with travelers.

61
00:08:28,840 --> 00:08:34,280
More than 30 airports across the country now have therapy dogs.

62
00:08:34,280 --> 00:08:36,660
San Francisco has a therapy pig.

63
00:08:36,660 --> 00:08:46,200
San Jose, California, began a dog program after the terrorist attacks of September 11.

64
00:08:46,200 --> 00:08:53,880
Since its beginning, the program has now grown and has 21 therapy dogs and a therapy cat.

65
00:08:53,880 --> 00:08:56,300
The animals don't get startled.

66
00:08:56,300 --> 00:09:04,480
They have had hundreds of hours of airport training, so they are used to having luggage and people crowding around them.

67
00:09:04,480 --> 00:09:12,820
These professional animals are probably better at finding their way in the airports than the most frequent of travelers.

68
00:09:12,820 --> 00:09:21,440
The passengers often say that seeing animals makes them feel much better and helps them to calm down before a flight.

69
00:09:21,440 --> 00:09:26,420
This little bit of support can sometimes make a big difference.

70
00:09:26,420 --> 00:09:34,600
Some passengers enjoy the animals so much that they call the airport to schedule flights around their visits.

71
00:09:34,600 --> 00:09:41,160
Visits to nursing homes and schools are also a regular part of the horses' schedule.

72
00:09:41,160 --> 00:09:55,143
Their owner is already working on a new idea for a therapy animal--donkeys.

73
00:09:55,143 --> 00:10:14,120
Q9. What is special about the Cincinnati/Northern Kentucky International Airport?

74
00:10:14,120 --> 00:10:33,120
Q10. What are the therapy animals at the airport trained to do?

75
00:10:33,120 --> 00:10:43,065
Q11. What do some passengers do to see the therapy animals?

76
00:10:43,065 --> 00:10:45,159
PASSAGE 2
Hello, viewers.

77
00:10:45,159 --> 00:10:51,440
Today I'm standing at a two-thousand-year-old Roman-era site.

78
00:10:51,440 --> 00:11:02,800
Here the brightly colored scenes that once decorated a mansion are being dug up.

79
00:11:02,800 --> 00:11:13,540
These scenes are turning up in the southern French city of Arles, surprising the historians who have been working here since 2014.

80
00:11:13,540 --> 00:11:18,760
Patches of paint still cling to the stone walls of the bedroom and reception hall.

81
00:11:18,760 --> 00:11:24,320
Some of these painted walls are preserved in places to a height of one meter.

82
00:11:24,320 --> 00:11:29,980
In addition, thousands of fragments that fell off the walls have been recovered.

83
00:11:29,980 --> 00:11:37,240
These pieces have been put back together with great care and display a variety of images.

84
00:11:37,240 --> 00:11:48,680
Some of these images include figures never seen before in France, such as a woman playing a stringed instrument, possibly a character from mythology.

85
00:11:48,680 --> 00:11:57,240
The paintings were done with such skill and with such expensive dyes that experts believe the artist originally came from Italy.

86
00:11:57,240 --> 00:12:24,260
They were likely hired by one of the city's elite, perhaps a Roman official wanted to make his home more beautiful, or maybe a wealthy local wanted to show off his worldly sophistication.

87
00:12:24,260 --> 00:12:33,760
The paintings may yield even more stunning surprises as additional sections are put together, like pieces of a puzzle.

88
00:12:33,760 --> 00:12:58,209
Whoever it was that created such magnificent pieces of art, they surely had no idea that their work would still be around thousands of years later.

89
00:12:58,209 --> 00:13:11,374
Q12. Where is the speaker standing?

90
00:13:11,374 --> 00:13:29,240
Q13. What have the historians found after putting the fragments together?

91
00:13:29,240 --> 00:13:48,280
Q14. What impresses the experts about the paintings?

92
00:13:48,280 --> 00:14:36,340
Q15. What do we learn about the artist who created the paintings?

93
00:14:36,340 --> 00:14:39,200
RECORDING 1
Good afternoon, class.

94
00:14:39,200 --> 00:14:43,980
Today I want to discuss with you a new approach to empirical research.

95
00:14:43,980 --> 00:14:48,780
In the past, scientists often worked alone.

96
00:14:48,780 --> 00:14:53,760
They were confined to the university or research center where they worked.

97
00:14:53,760 --> 00:15:02,000
Today, though, we are seeing mergers of some of the greatest scientific minds, regardless of their location.

98
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:07,780
There has never been a better time for collaborations with foreign scientists.

99
00:15:07,780 --> 00:15:12,140
In fact, the European Union is taking the lead.

100
00:15:12,140 --> 00:15:25,820
Spurred on by funding policies, half of European research articles had international co-authors in 2007, this is more than twice the level of two decades ago.

101
00:15:25,820 --> 00:15:35,560
The European Union's level of international co-authorship is about twice that of the United States, Japan, and India.

102
00:15:35,560 --> 00:15:40,440
Even so, the levels in these countries are also rising.

103
00:15:40,440 --> 00:15:49,180
This is a sign of the continued allure of creating scientific coalitions across borders.

104
00:15:49,180 --> 00:15:59,660
Andrew Schubert, a researcher at the Institute for Science Policy Research, says that the rising collaboration is partly out of necessity.

105
00:15:59,660 --> 00:16:03,300
This necessity comes with the rise of big science.

106
00:16:03,300 --> 00:16:07,560
Many scientific endeavors have become more complicated.

107
00:16:07,560 --> 00:16:12,880
These new complications require the money and labor of many nations.

108
00:16:12,880 --> 00:16:25,700
But he says collaborations have also emerged because of increased possibilities: the Internet allows like-minded scientists to find each other.

109
00:16:25,700 --> 00:16:35,360
Simultaneously, dramatic drops in communication costs ease long-distance interactions, and there's a reward.

110
00:16:35,360 --> 00:17:55,640
Studies of citation counts show that internationally co-authored papers have better visibility and are likely to be cited more often than papers written by a single researcher.

111
00:17:55,640 --> 00:18:03,030
However, there are challenges.

112
00:18:03,030 --> 00:18:23,354
For example, different countries have different regulations regarding intellectual property rights.

113
00:18:23,354 --> 00:18:38,134
Also, language barriers can sometimes make communication difficult.

114
00:18:38,134 --> 00:19:04,000
But despite these challenges, the trend towards international collaboration in science is clearly growing.

115
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:10,291
Q16. What do we learn about the European Union's scientific research?

116
00:19:10,291 --> 00:19:16,251
Q17. Why is international collaboration becoming increasingly common in science?

117
00:19:16,251 --> 00:19:21,880
Q18. What is a challenge facing international scientific collaboration?

118
00:19:21,880 --> 00:19:24,540
RECORDING 2
Good evening.

119
00:19:24,540 --> 00:19:35,980
In 1959, on the day that I was born, a headline in Life magazine proclaimed "Target Venus: There May be Life There!"

120
00:19:35,980 --> 00:20:01,940
It told of how scientists rode a balloon to an altitude of 80,000 feet to make telescope observations of Venus's atmosphere, and how their discovery of water raised hopes that there could be living things there.

121
00:20:01,940 --> 00:20:04,562
For many of my generation, Venus was the ultimate symbol of romance.

122
00:20:04,562 --> 00:20:07,402
It was the brightest object in the night sky, except for the moon.

123
00:20:07,402 --> 00:20:11,552
And it was often called the Earth's twin because it's about the same size and mass as our planet.

124
00:20:11,552 --> 00:20:14,174
But as we've learned more about Venus, that romantic image has faded.

125
00:20:14,174 --> 00:20:16,140
Today, we know that Venus is a hellish place.

126
00:20:16,140 --> 00:20:18,106
Its surface temperature is hot enough to melt lead.

127
00:20:18,106 --> 00:20:20,509
Its atmosphere is 96% carbon dioxide, with clouds of sulfuric acid.

128
00:20:20,509 --> 00:20:23,131
The atmospheric pressure at the surface is 92 times that of Earth.

129
00:20:23,131 --> 00:20:27,937
But despite all this, there are still some scientists who believe that Venus could have been a habitable planet in the past.

130
00:20:27,937 --> 00:20:32,525
Recent studies suggest that Venus may have had a shallow ocean of liquid water on its surface for billions of years.

131
00:20:32,525 --> 00:20:36,020
It may have had a climate similar to Earth's, with temperatures that were comfortable for life.

132
00:20:36,020 --> 00:20:43,683
But something happened about 700 million years ago that caused a runaway greenhouse effect.

133
00:20:43,683 --> 00:20:49,157
The planet's surface temperature rose dramatically, and the oceans evaporated.

134
00:20:49,157 --> 00:20:56,820
Scientists are now planning new missions to Venus to learn more about its history.

135
00:20:56,820 --> 00:21:51,709
They want to find out if there really was life on Venus, and if so, what happened to it.

136
00:21:51,709 --> 00:22:40,820
These missions could also help us better understand the effects of climate change on our own planet.

137
00:22:40,820 --> 00:23:00,474
Q19. What did the Life magazine headline say about Venus in 1959?

138
00:23:00,474 --> 00:23:17,180
Q20. Why was Venus once called the Earth's twin?

139
00:23:17,180 --> 00:23:37,260
Q21. What do some scientists believe about Venus?

140
00:23:37,260 --> 00:23:42,880
RECORDING 3
I'm a psychology professor at the University of British Columbia.

141
00:23:42,880 --> 00:23:51,520
I specialize in cultural psychology, examining similarities and differences between East Asians and North Americans.

142
00:23:51,520 --> 00:24:07,780
Our research team has been looking at cultural differences in self-enhancing motivation, helping people have positive feelings towards not only themselves but things connected to themselves.

143
00:24:07,780 --> 00:24:14,780
For example, when you own something, you view it as more valuable than when you don't own it.

144
00:24:14,780 --> 00:24:17,640
It's called the endowment effect.

145
00:24:17,640 --> 00:24:24,760
The strength of that effect is stronger in Western cultures than in East Asian cultures.

146
00:24:24,760 --> 00:24:35,280
So we've been looking at other ways of seeing whether this motivation to view oneself positively is shaped by cultural experiences.

147
00:24:35,280 --> 00:24:39,800
We've also started to look at how culture shapes sleep.

148
00:24:39,800 --> 00:24:54,380
We are still in the exploratory stages of this project, although what's noteworthy is that East Asians on average sleep about an hour and a half less each night than North Americans do.

149
00:24:54,380 --> 00:25:02,460
And it's not a more efficient sleep, not like they're compressing relatively more value out of their hours.

150
00:25:02,460 --> 00:25:09,960
Studies have found that even infants in East Asia sleep about an hour less than European infants.

151
00:25:09,960 --> 00:25:16,200
So we're trying to figure out how culture shapes the way you sleep.

152
00:25:16,200 --> 00:25:20,420
Our experiment does not take place in a sleep lab.

153
00:25:20,420 --> 00:25:26,311
Instead, we lend people motion-detecting watches and they wear them for a week at a time, whenever they are not having a shower or swimming.

154
00:25:26,311 --> 00:25:29,936
These watches record movement, so we can determine when someone is asleep and when they're awake.

155
00:25:29,936 --> 00:25:34,920
We also ask people to keep sleep diaries, where they write down when they go to bed and when they wake up.

156
00:25:34,920 --> 00:25:54,295
So far, our results suggest that cultural differences in sleep are real.

157
00:25:54,295 --> 00:26:10,440
But we still don't know exactly why these differences exist.

158
00:26:10,440 --> 00:26:14,594
Is it because of differences in work schedules?

159
00:26:14,594 --> 00:26:19,268
Or is it because of differences in family structures?

160
00:26:19,268 --> 00:26:22,384
Or is it something else entirely?

161
00:26:22,384 --> 00:26:27,058
We hope to answer these questions in future studies.

162
00:26:27,058 --> 00:26:35,367
Q22. What is the speaker's area of specialization?

163
00:26:35,367 --> 00:26:46,272
Q23. What do we learn about East Asians' sleep compared to North Americans'?

164
00:26:46,272 --> 00:26:55,620
Q24. How does the speaker's team conduct their sleep research?

165
00:26:55,620 --> 00:27:51,079
Q25. What does the speaker say about their sleep research?
